WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:03.490
In the supplied model, the sea is on the left and

00:00:03.490 --> 00:00:04.530
land is on the right.

00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:08.690
A transgression moves the shoreline landward as relative sea level rises,

00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:11.530
whether the water rises or the land subsides.

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:14.550
Beach sand covers an earlier erosion surface.

00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:18.570
Follow one fixed location while neighboring environments move across it.

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:21.830
At the fixed location, an early beach is followed by a

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:23.670
lagoon and then an offshore reef.

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:28.710
The preserved order is beach sandstone, lagoon mudstone and reef limestone,

00:00:28.970 --> 00:00:31.610
from oldest at the bottom to youngest at the top.

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:34.390
Different environments also coexist laterally.

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:37.350
Their sedimentary expressions are called facies.

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:42.650
During regression, the shoreline and neighboring facies shift seaward, toward the

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:47.270
left in this source. Lagoon mud spreads over earlier reef limestone,

00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:50.090
and beach or dune sand advances over mud.

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:54.030
Previously deposited material can become exposed and reworked.

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:58.450
New layers accumulate above the older transgressive package rather than replacing

00:00:58.450 --> 00:00:59.630
its entire history.

00:01:00.970 --> 00:01:05.870
The example transgression records sandstone below mudstone below limestone.

00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:09.570
Its regressive succession reverses the environmental trend:

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:12.310
limestone, then mudstone, then sandstone.

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:17.170
Those particular rocks belong to this illustrated setting, not every coastline.

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:21.590
Direction of shoreline migration and independent environmental clues carry the interpretation.

00:01:22.190 --> 00:01:24.150
A layer need not continue forever.

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:27.790
A facies can thin out where its depositional environment ends, and

00:01:27.790 --> 00:01:30.650
a channel deposit may be restricted to the channel itself.

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:32.850
Erosion can also remove material.

00:01:33.110 --> 00:01:37.410
Before correlating two exposures, consider both lateral facies differences and later

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:41.270
removal rather than assuming a missing bed was never deposited anywhere.
